spirv: warn when a global atomic has no storage buffer to route to

A global atomic is routed to the storage buffer whose range holds its address
(define_body). With no candidate buffer in the program, the routed function
tests nothing and returns zero, so every such atomic silently did nothing:
histograms, counters and exposure sums stayed empty. define_body now returns how
many candidate buffers it tests, and an atomic function built with none logs a
counted warning (the first 16, then every power of two). Loads and writes are
unchanged.

